talk(265,'1972-04-03','A. Yu. Geronimus','P000430-P000436','Sites, sheaves, derived cohomology and the classifying topos',r'''Following a minimum reading programme on topology, descent and simplicial methods, Geronimus develops covering families, sheaves, adjunction, derived cohomology and a Leray spectral sequence. He then constructs a classifying site for group objects and applies the language to Quillen's finite-field cohomology calculation. An exercise refers broadly to Artin on formal and algebraic objects, without stating a theorem. The exposition revisits exact earlier occurrences such as 1967-143.2, 1967-143.5 and 1970-226.2--3; no new problem identifier is created for those definitions.''')
item(265,'P000430-P000431','definition','Grothendieck covering families and fibre products',r'''Coverings are families \(\{X_\alpha\to X\}\), stable under isomorphisms, composition and arbitrary base change. The base-change axiom explicitly requires existence of \(X_\alpha\times_X U\); a universal diagram defines the fibre product.''',r'''A pretopology on a category \(T\) assigns covering families satisfying the three stated axioms. A fibre product \(U\times_XV\) represents pairs of arrows to \(U,V\) with equal composites to \(X\). The associated Grothendieck topology consists of sieves containing a covering family. The distinction between a pretopology and its topology matters when different covering families generate the same sheaves.''',r'''The modern term pretopology and the associated-sieve construction are supplied; the manuscript's axioms themselves are accurate.''','E',r'''The framework was circulated in SGA 4 in 1963--64 and published in 1972 \citep{sga41972topos}; a precise current formulation is \citet[Tag 00VH]{stacks2026sites}.''',r'''the definitions are standard and recur from the seminar's earlier years.''',['sga41972topos','stacks2026sites'],correct='correct',area='Algebraic geometry')
item(265,'P000431-P000432','definition','Presheaves, natural transformations and the functor category',r'''A presheaf is a contravariant functor \(T\to A\), with \(A\) abelian. A commuting square defines a morphism of functors; the notes observe a possible set-theoretic size issue.''',r'''For a small site \(T\), an abelian presheaf is a functor \(T^{\rm op}\to\mathbf{Ab}\). A morphism consists of maps \(t_U:F(U)\to G(U)\) commuting with every restriction map. These form an abelian functor category with kernels and cokernels computed objectwise. For a large site, one fixes a universe or a suitable small presentation before asserting that morphism collections are sets.''',r'''The coefficient category is restricted to abelian groups in the statements used below. Mere existence of infinite direct sums in an arbitrary abelian category is not a complete substitute for all limits and exactness conditions needed in the subsequent sheaf theory.''','E',r'''This is the categorical language of \citet[Chapter I]{grothendieck1957homological} and \citet[Expos\'e I]{sga41972topos}. The seminar is building shared terminology.''',r'''these constructions are standard; the manuscript's warning about size is appropriate.''',['grothendieck1957homological','sga41972topos'],verification='publisher metadata checked',area='Category theory')
item(265,'P000431','definition','The sheaf condition uses products over an arbitrary cover',r'''The sheaf condition is displayed with \(\bigoplus_\alpha F(X_\alpha)\) and \(\bigoplus_{\alpha,\beta}F(X_\alpha\times_X X_\beta)\), although the covering index is an arbitrary set.''',r'''For every covering \(\{U_i\to U\}\), an abelian presheaf is a sheaf when \[0\longrightarrow F(U)\longrightarrow\prod_iF(U_i)\xrightarrow{\operatorname{pr}_1^*-\operatorname{pr}_2^*}\prod_{i,j}F(U_i\times_UU_j)\] is exact. Products and direct sums coincide for finite covers, but direct sums impose an unwarranted finite-support condition on infinite covers.''',r'''The products are an emendation of the mathematical statement, not a silent change to the diplomatic transcription. For an infinite discrete space covered by its points, a global locally constant function need not have finite support.''','E',r'''The exact equalizer formulation is \citet[Tag 00VM]{stacks2026sites}; it belongs to the pre-existing sheaf formalism of SGA 4.''',r'''the arbitrary-cover statement in the notebook is false with direct sums and correct with products.''',['stacks2026sites','sga41972topos'],correct='false as written',development='annotated',area='Algebraic geometry')
item(265,'P000432','theorem','Sheafification, adjunction and the abelian category of sheaves',r'''The full subcategory \(S_T\) of sheaves is abelian. The proof uses an associated-sheaf functor \(\#\), adjoint to the inclusion \(i:S_T\to P_T\). Adjointness is defined by a natural Hom-set isomorphism.''',r'''For abelian presheaves on a small site, sheafification \(a:P_T\to S_T\) is an exact left adjoint to the inclusion: \(\operatorname{Hom}(aF,G)\cong\operatorname{Hom}(F,iG)\), naturally in both variables. The category \(S_T\) is abelian; kernels are computed as presheaf kernels, whereas cokernels must be sheafified. A Hom-set bijection without naturality would not define an adjunction.''',r'''The direction of the adjunction and exactness of sheafification are explicit. The conclusion is stated for abelian groups, the coefficient category actually needed later.''','E',r'''These results are in the classical site formalism \citep{sga41972topos}; \citet[Tag 03CM, Lemmas 18.3.1--2]{stacks2026foundations} verifies the exact current statements.''',r'''the theorem and proof mechanism are standard; inclusion of sheaves into presheaves is generally only left exact.''',['sga41972topos','stacks2026foundations'],area='Algebraic geometry')
item(265,'P000433','definition','Cohomology on a site as a derived section functor',r'''The definition is \(H^q(T,X,F)=R^qi(F)(X)\), where \(i\) includes sheaves in presheaves.''',r'''For an object \(X\) of a small site and an abelian sheaf \(F\), define \(H^q(X,F)=R^q\Gamma(X,-)(F)\). Abelian sheaves have enough injectives. Evaluation of presheaves at \(X\) is exact, so computing an injective resolution also gives \(H^q(X,F)=(R^qi(F))(X)\).''',r'''Enough injectives and the exactness of evaluation explain why the notebook's seemingly unusual inclusion-functor definition agrees with ordinary site cohomology.''','E',r'''The homological construction predates the talk \citep{grothendieck1957homological}; the site version and enough-injectives theorem are verified in \citet[Tags 01DL and 03NT]{stacks2026foundations}.''',r'''the definition is correct for abelian sheaves on a site, with standard size conventions.''',['grothendieck1957homological','stacks2026foundations'],correct='correct',area='Algebraic geometry')
item(265,'P000433-P000434','claim','Continuity of a functor of sites',r'''A morphism of topologies is defined as a functor carrying coverings to coverings; precomposition \(F\mapsto F\circ f\) is asserted to carry sheaves to sheaves.''',r'''If \(f:T_1\to T_2\) carries covering families to covering families and preserves the fibre products that occur in their descent diagrams, then precomposition carries sheaves on \(T_2\) to sheaves on \(T_1\). More generally continuity is defined by that sheaf-preservation condition. Cover preservation alone does not justify substituting \(f(U_i\times_UU_j)\) for \(f(U_i)\times_{f(U)}f(U_j)\).''',r'''The missing fibre-product compatibility is supplied. The direction and notation in the notes are retained as precomposition, without identifying this automatically with the exact inverse-image functor of a geometric morphism of topoi.''','E',r'''The distinction is part of \citet[Expos\'e III]{sga41972topos}. Its 1963--64 circulation predates the seminar; the volume appeared in 1972.''',r'''the construction is standard under continuity hypotheses, while the displayed definition is insufficient as written.''',['sga41972topos'],correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',area='Algebraic geometry')
item(265,'P000433-P000435','theorem','Grothendieck and Leray spectral sequences',r'''For \(A\xrightarrow F B\xrightarrow G C\), the notes state \(R^pG\,R^qF\Rightarrow R^{p+q}(GF)\) ``under certain conditions'', and present the site Leray sequence as a special case.''',r'''Let \(A,B,C\) be abelian categories, with enough injectives in \(A,B\). Suppose \(F,G\) are additive left exact functors and \(F\) carries injectives to \(G\)-acyclic objects. For \(M\in A\), there is a first-quadrant spectral sequence \(E_2^{p,q}=R^pG(R^qF(M))\Rightarrow R^{p+q}(GF)(M)\). For a morphism of topoi \(f\), this yields \(H^p(Y,R^qf_*F)\Rightarrow H^{p+q}(X,F)\).''',r'''The acyclicity assumption is the decisive missing condition. The modern Leray formulation uses the direct-image functor \(f_*\); the manuscript's opposite site-functor conventions must be translated before comparing symbols.''','E',r'''The composite-functor spectral sequence is due to the earlier homological formalism \citep{grothendieck1957homological}; its hypotheses and convergence are verified in \citet[Tag 015L, Lemma 13.22.2]{stacks2026foundations}.''',r'''the spectral sequence is established. Exactness of precomposition on presheaves alone does not replace the injective-acyclicity check on sheaves.''',['grothendieck1957homological','stacks2026foundations'],area='Homological algebra')
item(265,'P000434-P000435','construction','Group objects and the classifying site',r'''A group object is described by a functorial group structure on \(h_G(Y)\), or by multiplication, inversion and a unit. The new site \(TG\) consists of \(G\)-objects, equivariant maps and underlying coverings; its final object is called a classifying space.''',r'''In a category with finite products, a group object is an object \(G\) equipped with the group-law diagrams. Yoneda identifies this with a group structure on every \(\operatorname{Hom}(Y,G)\), natural in \(Y\). For a group object in a topos \(T\), the topos of \(G\)-objects is its classifying topos \(B_TG\). Its final object is the base's final object with trivial action; the topology and equivariance, not a nontrivial underlying terminal set, encode classifying cohomology.''',r'''The classifying topos is distinguished from a topological classifying space. For a discrete group over the topos of sets, abelian objects are \(G\)-modules and derived global sections recover group cohomology.''','E',r'''The construction belongs to \citet[Expos\'e IV]{sga41972topos}. It is the conceptual language needed for the subsequent comparison, not a new construction of ordinary \(BG\) from a terminal object.''',r'''the classifying-topos interpretation is standard; the notebook's terminal-object phrase is too compressed if read as a statement about ordinary spaces.''',['sga41972topos'],match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',area='Algebraic geometry')
item(265,'P000435','definition','Constant sheaves and the local coefficients in a fibration sequence',r'''The constant sheaf associated with \(A\) is correctly obtained from the constant presheaf. A later display simplifies the \(E_2\) page to \(H^p(BG)\otimes H^q(G)\).''',r'''The constant sheaf \(\underline A\) is the sheafification of the constant presheaf with value \(A\). For a Serre fibration \(F\to E\to B\) with connected CW base, the cohomology spectral sequence has \(E_2^{p,q}=H^p(B;\mathcal H^q(F;k))\), where monodromy defines the coefficient system. Over a field, trivial monodromy and suitable finiteness hypotheses identify this with \(H^p(B;k)\otimes H^q(F;k)\).''',r'''The fibration version is a precise model of the analogy drawn in the notes. Trivial local coefficients and tensor-product hypotheses are supplied; no general tensor-product formula for arbitrary sites or coefficient rings is asserted.''','E',r'''Constant sheaves and site Leray sequences are already present in SGA 4 \citep{sga41972topos}; the classical fibration sequence is the topological analogy used by the speaker.''',r'''the constant-sheaf construction is correct. The tensor notation needs the additional conditions expressly alluded to but not specified in the notes.''',['sga41972topos'],match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',area='Topology')
item(265,'P000436','theorem','Quillen’s mod-l cohomology of a finite general linear group',r'''The notes write \(H^*(GL_n(\mathbb F_q);\mathbb F_\ell)=S[c_1,\ldots,c_n]\otimes\Lambda[c'_1,\ldots,c'_n]\), with degrees \(2i\) and \(2i-1\), but do not state the relation between \(q\) and \(\ell\). One coefficient field is also written \(\mathbb F_q\).''',r'''For an odd prime \(\ell\neq\operatorname{char}\mathbb F_q\), let \(r\) be the multiplicative order of \(q\) modulo \(\ell\), and \(m=\lfloor n/r\rfloor\). Quillen's description is \[H^*(GL_n(\mathbb F_q);\mathbb F_\ell)\cong\mathbb F_\ell[c_r,c_{2r},\ldots,c_{mr}]\otimes\Lambda(e_r,e_{2r},\ldots,e_{mr}),\] with \(|c_{jr}|=2jr\), \(|e_{jr}|=2jr-1\). The displayed notebook formula is the case \(r=1\), namely \(\ell\mid q-1\). The prime \(2\) requires separate cases.''',r'''The odd-prime restrictions and the order \(r\) supply the absent hypotheses. The coefficient of every cohomology group in this formula is \(\mathbb F_\ell\).''','B',r'''The result is published in \citet{quillen1972finitefields}, later in 1972. The notebook demonstrates that the calculation was already being discussed before publication; the exact theorem text was not available in the publisher page used here.''',r'''the formula with these qualifications is established. The unrestricted printed formula is false, for example when \(\ell\nmid|GL_n(\mathbb F_q)|\), when all positive-degree cohomology vanishes.''',['quillen1972finitefields'],correct='false as written',verification='publisher metadata checked',confidence='medium',area='Algebraic topology')
item(265,'P000436','method','Finite generation through a faithful unitary representation',r'''A faithful irreducible representation \(G\to U\) and the induced \(BG\to BU\) are invoked to explain finite generation of group cohomology.''',r'''A finite group has a faithful finite-dimensional unitary representation, for example its regular representation. It need not have a faithful irreducible representation. The classical finite-generation theorem says that \(H^*(G;\mathbb F_\ell)\) is a finitely generated algebra for every finite group \(G\); the topological proof uses \(G\subset U(N)\) and the fibration \(U(N)/G\to BG\to BU(N)\).''',r'''Irreducibility is removed; it is false for a noncyclic finite abelian group. The coefficient ring and finiteness of \(G\) are stated explicitly.''','E',r'''The finite-generation theorem was proved before this seminar by the Evens--Venkov methods; \citet{evens1961cohomology} is the earlier algebraic proof. The notebook itself marks this fact as known.''',r'''finite generation is established. It gives no automatic explicit set of Quillen generators or a Brauer-lift comparison.''',['evens1961cohomology'],correct='false as written',verification='publisher metadata checked',confidence='medium',area='Algebraic topology')
item(265,'P000436','claim','Comparing finite-field and complex algebraic groups',r'''Passing from \(GL_n(\mathbb F_q)\) to \(GL_n(\overline{\mathbb F}_q)\), the notes say the resulting classifying object, properly interpreted, equals \(BU\). Bernstein adds that representations over the algebraic closure and over \(\mathbb C\) are described by highest weights.''',r'''The comparison concerns étale or prime-to-characteristic completed homotopy/cohomology of algebraic groups and their classifying objects, not an equality between the discrete classifying space of \(GL_n(\overline{\mathbb F}_q)\) and the ordinary topological \(BU(n)\). For a split connected reductive group over an algebraically closed field, simple rational representations are indexed by dominant highest weights. In positive characteristic their dimensions, characters and semisimplicity properties need not agree with characteristic zero.''',r'''The topology, finite rank and coefficient restrictions are restored. The highest-weight observation is separated from the much stronger and false assertion that the two representation categories coincide.''','E',r'''The étale comparison is the background of \citet{artinmazur1969etale} and Quillen's application \citep{quillen1972finitefields}. The highest-weight parametrization is standard reductive-group theory; see \citet[Chapter 22]{milne2017groups}.''',r'''these qualified comparisons are established; neither literal equality of spaces nor characteristic-independent ordinary representation theory follows from the notebook.''',['artinmazur1969etale','quillen1972finitefields','milne2017groups'],match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',confidence='medium',area='Algebraic geometry')
corr('P000431','sheaf equalizer','Direct sums over arbitrary covering families','Products over the covering family and the pairwise fibre products','The scan shows direct-sum signs. For infinite covers the sheaf axiom uses products.');
corr('P000433','continuity of site functor','Cover-preserving implies precomposition preserves sheaves','Add preservation of the relevant fibre products or the general continuity condition','Applying the sheaf equalizer requires identifying the overlaps after applying the functor.');
corr('P000436','Quillen cohomology formula','All primes and all q give n polynomial and n exterior generators','For odd ell use indices divisible by ord_ell(q); the displayed indices require ell dividing q-1','The scan supplies no condition on ell. Prime 2 also has separate cases.');
corr('P000436','degree of c_i','c_i in H^{2i}(G,F_q)','c_i in H^{2i}(G,F_ell)','The surrounding formula and companion exterior generators use F_ell; mathematical coefficient correction.');
corr('P000436','unitary embedding','faithful irreducible representation','faithful finite-dimensional unitary representation','A finite noncyclic abelian group has no faithful irreducible representation.');save()

talk(266,'1972-04-10','A. Yu. Geronimus','P000437-P000438','The homotopy category and localization at quasi-isomorphisms',r'''Geronimus builds the derived category from complexes and homotopies, emphasizes its universal property, and explicitly rejects identifying maps merely because they induce the same map on cohomology. A roof construction follows, although the notebook mistakenly calls the resulting limit projective. The examples begin in nonnegative degrees. A crossed-out reprise on P000439 belongs to this discussion but adds no distinct live assertion.''')
item(266,'P000437','definition','Complexes, cohomology, degree and homotopy',r'''A complex \(0\to C_0\xrightarrow{d_0}C_1\to\cdots\) has \(H^q=\ker d_q/\operatorname{im}d_{q-1}\). Maps of degree \(\tau\) are introduced and a homotopy formula \(f-g=d\alpha+\alpha d'\) is written without consistent indices or signs.''',r'''For a cochain complex \(C^\bullet\) in an abelian category, \(d^{n+1}d^n=0\) and \(H^n(C)=\ker d^n/\operatorname{im}d^{n-1}\). Degree-zero chain maps \(f,g:C\to D\) are homotopic if \(f-g=d_Dh+hd_C\), with \(h^n:C^n\to D^{n-1}\). In the graded Hom complex a degree-\(\tau\) map has differential \(d_Df-(-1)^\tau fd_C\); the corresponding signed boundary formula governs higher-degree homotopies.''',r'''The degree-zero statement makes the notebook's intended homotopy unambiguous. The graded sign convention is explicitly supplied rather than treating the unsiged formula as correct in every degree.''','E',r'''These are the classical chain-complex definitions, incorporated in the derived-category formalism before 1972; see \citet[Chapter I]{hartshorne1966residues} and \citet[Chapter 12]{may1999concise}.''',r'''the definitions are standard; homotopic maps induce equal maps on cohomology.''',['hartshorne1966residues','may1999concise'],match='partial',verification='indirect',area='Homological algebra')
item(266,'P000437','definition','The homotopy category is generally not abelian',r'''Morphisms are maps modulo null-homotopic maps. The notes collect morphisms of all degrees into a direct sum, then stress that the resulting category is not abelian because of homotopy equivalence.''',r'''The ordinary homotopy category \(K(A)\) has complexes as objects and homotopy classes of degree-zero chain maps as morphisms. It is additive and triangulated, but generally not abelian. The groups \(\operatorname{Hom}_{K(A)}(C,D[\tau])\) form a graded enhancement; collecting every degree changes the morphism convention and should not be confused with ordinary \(K(A)\).''',r'''The text's all-degrees convention is separated from the standard category used in the subsequent localization. The abelian category of actual complexes and chain maps must also be distinguished from its homotopy category.''','E',r'''The distinction is already in \citet[Chapter I]{hartshorne1966residues}; the precise conventions are verified in \citet[Tag 03T3]{stacks2026foundations}.''',r'''the non-abelian homotopy-category statement is established, with the specified convention for morphisms.''',['hartshorne1966residues','stacks2026foundations'],correct='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Homological algebra')
item(266,'P000437-P000438','construction','The derived category and its universal property',r'''The functor \(K(A)\to D(A)\) sends cohomology isomorphisms to isomorphisms, is universal with this property, and in fact a chain map becomes invertible exactly when it induces cohomology isomorphisms.''',r'''Let \(S\) be the quasi-isomorphisms in \(K(A)\). The derived category is \(D(A)=K(A)[S^{-1}]\), with the localization universal property: every functor inverting \(S\) factors uniquely up to natural isomorphism through \(D(A)\). The cohomology functors descend to \(D(A)\); a map in \(K(A)\) is a quasi-isomorphism if and only if its localized image is invertible.''',r'''The relevant set-theoretic universe and existence of the localization are tacit; the bounded-below setting of the talk suffices in the standard module and sheaf categories.''','E',r'''The construction was available in the 1963--64 Harvard seminar published as \citet[Chapter I]{hartshorne1966residues}; \citet[Tag 03T3]{stacks2026foundations} verifies the current convention.''',r'''this is the standard derived category, and the universal property recorded in the notes is accurate.''',['hartshorne1966residues','stacks2026foundations'],correct='correct',area='Homological algebra')
item(266,'P000438','remark','Derived morphisms retain information invisible to cohomology',r'''The notes emphatically say that \(D(A)\) is not obtained by declaring \(f\sim g\) when \(f^*=g^*\); it has more morphisms, obtained by formally adjoining inverses.''',r'''In general the functor \(H^*:D(A)\to\operatorname{Gr}(A)\) is not faithful. For modules, \(\operatorname{Hom}_{D(R)}(M,N[1])\cong\operatorname{Ext}^1_R(M,N)\). For example \(R=\mathbb Z\), \(M=N=\mathbb Z/p\), this group is \(\mathbb Z/p\), whereas a morphism \(M\to N[1]\) induces zero on each cohomology group because the nonzero groups lie in different degrees.''',r'''The Ext example is an editorial illustration of the correct warning in the notebook; it is not presented as an example supplied by the speaker.''','E',r'''This is an elementary consequence of the earlier derived-functor formalism \citep[Chapter I]{hartshorne1966residues}.''',r'''the warning is correct and essential: cohomology detects quasi-isomorphisms without determining every morphism.''',['hartshorne1966residues'],correct='correct',match='partial',verification='indirect',development='annotated',area='Homological algebra')
item(266,'P000438','construction','Roofs and the direction of the limit',r'''Morphisms from \(C\) to \(D\) are represented by diagrams \(C\leftarrow C'\to D\), with the left arrow a cohomology isomorphism. The notes call the set of such morphisms a projective limit.''',r'''A morphism in \(D(A)\) is represented by a roof \(C\xleftarrow{s}C'\xrightarrow{f}D\), with \(s\) a quasi-isomorphism, modulo the calculus-of-fractions equivalence by common refinements in \(K(A)\). The Hom set is a filtered colimit of the corresponding Hom sets over quasi-isomorphic replacements, with the appropriate opposite indexing category. It is not the inverse limit of all roof diagrams.''',r'''The homotopy-category equivalence relation and the direction of the colimit are supplied. The word \ru{проект. предел} is visible in the scan and is treated as a mathematical emendation, not an English translation mistake.''','E',r'''Roofs are the standard localization construction of \citet[Chapter I]{hartshorne1966residues}; they implement the universal property described correctly earlier on the same page.''',r'''the roof construction is established, while the unqualified projective-limit statement needs correction.''',['hartshorne1966residues'],correct='false as written',match='partial',verification='indirect',development='annotated',area='Homological algebra')
item(266,'P000438','construction','When an additive functor has a total derived functor',r'''The concluding line sends \(f:A\to A'\) to \(D(f):D(A)\to D(A')\), without stating exactness or resolution hypotheses.''',r'''An exact additive functor preserves quasi-isomorphisms and induces a functor on derived categories. For a left exact additive functor \(F:A\to A'\), with enough injectives in \(A\), the bounded-below right derived functor is \(RF:D^+(A)\to D^+(A')\), computed by applying \(F\) to an injective resolution. A general additive functor has no automatic induced functor on the unlocalized cohomology classes.''',r'''The two cases, exact functor and right derived functor, are distinguished. Kazhdan's next intervention supplies the missing injective-resolution mechanism.''','E',r'''The construction is classical; \citet[Tag 03T3, Definition 64.6.4]{stacks2026foundations} verifies exactly the bounded-below formulation.''',r'''derived functors are standard under these hypotheses; the unrestricted displayed notation suppresses necessary assumptions.''',['stacks2026foundations','hartshorne1966residues'],area='Homological algebra')
corr('P000437','maps of arbitrary degree','f-g=d alpha+alpha d-prime without graded signs','For degree zero use d_D h+h d_C; for degree tau use the graded Hom differential','The displayed arbitrary degree requires a consistent signed and indexed convention.');
corr('P000438','roof construction limit','projective limit','Filtered colimit of Hom sets over quasi-isomorphic replacements, modulo roof equivalence','The scan visibly says projective limit; derived localization formally adds inverses and uses the calculus of fractions.');save()

talk(267,'1972-04-10','D. A. Kazhdan','P000439','Computing the derived category with injective complexes',r'''Kazhdan supplies the practical model omitted from the preceding abstract localization: enough injectives allow one to work with injective complexes. The adjacent complex-bordism lecture starts only after this short intervention. The omitted boundedness qualification is recoverable from Geronimus's nonnegative complexes on P000437.''')
item(267,'P000439','theorem','Bounded-below injective complexes model the derived category',r'''If \(A\) has enough injectives, the category of complexes in \(\operatorname{Inj}(A)\) is said to be equivalent to the derived category of \(A\).''',r'''For an abelian category \(A\) with enough injectives, the natural functor \(K^+(\operatorname{Inj}A)\to D^+(A)\) is an equivalence. Morphisms on the left are chain maps modulo homotopy, not arbitrary chain maps. For unbounded complexes, termwise injectivity is insufficient in general; one uses \(K\)-injective resolutions in categories where they exist.''',r'''Bounded-below complexes and passage to homotopy classes are restored. This is the precise result compatible with the preceding nonnegative complexes.''','E',r'''The result belongs to the pre-1972 derived-category theory \citep{hartshorne1966residues}; \citet[Tag 03T3, Lemma 64.6.3]{stacks2026foundations} states the exact equivalence.''',r'''the bounded-below theorem is established. Its unbounded analogue needs the stronger \(K\)-injective condition.''',['hartshorne1966residues','stacks2026foundations'],area='Homological algebra')
save()

talk(268,'1972-04-10','Jack Morava','P000439-P000445','Complex bordism, formal groups and the emerging height picture',r'''Morava starts from geometric bordism and interprets the complex-bordism module of a space as a sheaf on the scheme of formal group laws. Coordinate changes, height strata, periodic theories and division-algebra stabilizers give an early form of chromatic geometry. Lazard and Quillen supply the older foundations; Morava's geometric programme is contemporary research, with a November 1972 unpublished announcement and later published developments. The strong claim that every fibre in one dense open set is integral \(K\)-theory requires substantial repair.''')
item(268,'P000439-P000440','definition','Unoriented, oriented and stably complex bordism',r'''Closed compact smooth manifolds are equivalent when they form the boundary of a compact manifold. Unoriented bordism has \([M]+[M]=0\). A weak complex manifold includes a complex bundle \(\xi\) and a fixed stable real-bundle isomorphism with the tangent bundle.''',r'''Unoriented bordism uses \(\partial W\cong M\sqcup N\) and disjoint-union addition. Oriented bordism requires \(\partial W\cong M\sqcup(-N)\). A stable complex structure on a smooth manifold is a complex structure on its stabilized tangent bundle, up to the standard stable homotopy equivalence; its choice induces an orientation and must extend across a bordism. It is data, not merely the existence of some isomorphism of underlying real bundles.''',r'''Boundary orientations and stable homotopies of the chosen structure are supplied. The cylinder proves exponent two only in unoriented bordism, not in complex bordism.''','E',r'''These geometric definitions precede the talk and are the input of \citet{connerfloyd1966relation} and the complex-bordism theory used in \citet{quillen1969formal}.''',r'''all three bordism theories are standard; keeping the extra structures prevents the false transfer of the unoriented exponent-two assertion.''',['connerfloyd1966relation','quillen1969formal'],verification='publisher metadata checked',area='Algebraic topology')
item(268,'P000440','claim','Representatives by complex manifolds: a necessary qualification',r'''The notes assert that every weak complex manifold is bordant to a complex manifold.''',r'''A safe precise result is that the complex bordism ring is generated, as a ring over \(\mathbb Z\), by classes of nonsingular projective varieties; hence each class is an integral linear combination of such classes. This does not mean that every class, with its specified stable complex orientation, is represented by a nonnegative disjoint union of complex manifolds. In dimension zero, \(MU_0\cong\mathbb Z\), and a negatively oriented point represents \(-1\), which no ordinary compact complex zero-manifold represents.''',r'''The generated-ring statement is the verified replacement. The zero-dimensional counterexample diagnoses the universal literal wording; no assertion about the sharp positive-dimensional realization theorem is inferred from this brief line.''','E',r'''The use of nonsingular projective generators is explicit in the proof of \citet[Theorem 2]{quillen1969formal}, relying on the earlier complex-bordism calculation.''',r'''the ring-generation result is established. The unqualified literal claim has a zero-dimensional counterexample.''',['quillen1969formal'],correct='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',area='Algebraic topology')
item(268,'P000441','theorem','The complex bordism coefficient ring',r'''The coefficient ring is written \(\Omega_*^U=\mathbb Z[t_0,t_1,\ldots]\), with \(\deg t_i=2i\). Products are Cartesian products, and inverses require changing the stable complex structure appropriately.''',r'''The complex bordism coefficient ring is \(MU_*\cong\mathbb Z[x_1,x_2,\ldots]\), where \(|x_i|=2i\) for \(i\geq1\); \(MU_0=\mathbb Z\) and \(MU_{2i+1}=0\). There is no independent degree-zero polynomial generator. Multiplication is induced by Cartesian product, with the product stable complex structure.''',r'''The subscript zero is removed as an independent generator; if \(t_0\) was intended to denote the point, it must equal the unit rather than a polynomial variable.''','E',r'''The Milnor--Novikov coefficient-ring calculation predates the talk; \citet[Theorem 2 and its proof]{quillen1969formal} identifies it with Lazard's polynomial ring and uses the projective generators.''',r'''the polynomial presentation is established; the notebook's extra degree-zero variable is erroneous if read literally.''',['quillen1969formal','lazard1955formal'],correct='false as written',area='Algebraic topology')
item(268,'P000441-P000442','construction','Bordism of a space and the module-classification programme',r'''A cycle over \(X\) is \([M,\xi,\alpha,f]\) with \(f:M\to X\), and a bordism extends all the data. The direct sum \(\Omega_*^U(X)\) is an \(\Omega_*^U\)-module. A problem asks to describe its module structure.''',r'''For a CW space \(X\), geometric complex bordism defines the homology theory \(MU_*(X)\). External products with coefficient manifolds make it a graded \(MU_*\)-module. The broad request to describe this module has no specified class of spaces or proposed classification invariant in the notes; it becomes a programme rather than a single quantified problem.''',r'''The covariant theory here is homology, despite later notation resembling \(K^*(X)\). No claim is made that all graded modules arise as \(MU_*(X)\), or that height data alone classify them.''','E',r'''Module structures were already a focus of complex-bordism theory before 1972. \citet[Introduction and Section 3]{johnsonwilson1975operations} makes the later height-local structure results precise and explicitly builds on Morava's 1971 unpublished work.''',r'''the module construction is standard, while the broad classification request is not a sufficiently bounded open problem to receive a new problem identifier.''',['johnsonwilson1975operations','connerfloyd1966relation'],match='partial',area='Algebraic topology')
item(268,'P000442','construction','Modules as quasicoherent sheaves and their fibres',r'''Ignoring most grading, the polynomial ring is viewed as the coordinate ring of an infinite affine space, and \(MU_*(X)\) as a sheaf; even and odd degrees are retained separately.''',r'''Put \(L=MU_*\) and \(M=MU_*(X)\). The associated quasicoherent sheaf \(\widetilde M\) on \(\operatorname{Spec}L\) has fibre \(M\otimes_L\kappa(\mathfrak p)\) at a prime \(\mathfrak p\). Forgetting to parity preserves a \(\mathbb Z/2\)-grading but discards the full weight information. Since \(L\) has infinitely many polynomial generators, this affine scheme is not noetherian.''',r'''The residue-field tensor product and the distinction between a stalk and its fibre are supplied. Fibres are vector spaces over varying residue fields, so they cannot literally all be the same integral \(K\)-group.''','E',r'''The module-to-sheaf construction is older affine algebraic geometry. Morava's use of it for formal groups is made explicit in \citet{morava1972moduli} and developed in \citet[Section 0.1]{morava1985noetherian}.''',r'''the construction is standard and gives the correct language for the following geometric claims, provided the grading and base field are tracked.''',['morava1972moduli','morava1985noetherian'],correct='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Algebraic topology')
item(268,'P000442','claim','The dense-open K-theory claim and the Conner–Floyd comparison',r'''A theorem attributed to Morava and Quillen says that every fibre over a dense open set is \(K^0(X)\oplus K^1(X)\); the open is identified with inverting \(t_1=[\mathbb {CP}^1]\).''',r'''For a finite CW complex, the Conner--Floyd comparison identifies \(MU_*(X)\otimes_{MU_*}KU_*\) with \(KU_*(X)\), where the coefficient map is the multiplicative formal group law and \(KU_*=\mathbb Z[\beta^{\pm1}]\). Inverting a degree-two polynomial generator in the universal ring does not impose that coefficient map. A geometric fibre at \(\mathfrak p\) is instead a vector space over \(\kappa(\mathfrak p)\); even at \(X=\mathrm{pt}\), it is not the integral group \(K^0(\mathrm{pt})=\mathbb Z\).''',r'''The comparison is stated in the covariant homology convention of the lecture. Passing to cohomology requires the corresponding finite-complex comparison, not an unmentioned identification of homology and cohomology. The height-one locus is defined prime by prime through the Hasse invariant, not by one universal \(t_1\) in all characteristics.''','E',r'''The correctly delimited comparison predates the talk \citep{connerfloyd1966relation}; the formal-group interpretation is explained by \citet{quillen1969formal}. Later height-local theory implements Morava's broader idea, but does not vindicate the literal fibre claim.''',r'''the Conner--Floyd theorem is established. The notebook's global dense-open fibre statement is false as written and should not be counted as an unsolved conjecture.''',['connerfloyd1966relation','quillen1969formal'],correct='false as written',match='partial',verification='indirect',development='annotated',area='Algebraic topology')
item(268,'P000442-P000444','construction','Formal coordinate changes and equivariant bordism data',r'''The group \(\Gamma(R)\) consists of \(f(t)=a_1t+a_2t^2+\cdots\), with \(a_1\) invertible. It acts by \(F^f(x,y)=f^{-1}F(f(x),f(y))\), and the bordism sheaf is called equivariant, ``constant on orbits''.''',r'''Formal substitutions with invertible linear term form a group under composition and act on one-dimensional commutative formal group laws by the displayed formula. Strict coordinate changes have \(a_1=1\). The flat Hopf algebroid \((MU_*,MU_*MU)\) encodes formal group laws and strict isomorphisms; the grading records the additional scaling. The comodule \(MU_*(X)\) therefore has descent data. Equivariance identifies fibres along chosen isomorphisms while allowing nontrivial stabilizer actions; it does not make the sheaf canonically constant on each orbit.''',r'''The strict/full-coordinate distinction and stabilizer action are supplied. A moduli stack with its grading is the accurate modern home for the informal quotient picture.''','B',r'''The coordinate action is recorded in Morava's unpublished announcement communicated on 22 November 1972 \citep{morava1972moduli}. Its comodule geometry appears in the published \citet[Sections 0.1 and 1.0]{morava1985noetherian}; Johnson--Wilson's 1975 paper already develops substantial parts of the programme.''',r'''the comodule and descent interpretation is established. The word ``constant'' is valid only in the weak sense of isomorphic fibres after choosing transport.''',['morava1972moduli','morava1985noetherian','johnsonwilson1975operations'],match='partial',area='Algebraic topology')
item(268,'P000443','theorem','Height classifies geometric orbits in fixed positive characteristic',r'''Orbits \(\mathcal D_{(p,h)}\) for primes \(p\) and heights \(h\), together with an infinite-height stratum, are proposed. The theory is said to depend on describing these orbits.''',r'''Over a fixed algebraically closed field \(k\) of characteristic \(p>0\), one-dimensional commutative formal groups are classified up to isomorphism by height \(h\in\{1,2,\ldots,\infty\}\), defined by the first nonzero term of \([p]_F(x)\), of degree \(p^h\); infinite height means \([p]_F=0\). The closure of the height-\(h\) stratum consists of heights at least \(h\). In characteristic zero all such groups become isomorphic to the additive group over a field. These are fibrewise orbit statements, not a claim that all points over \(\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z\) lie in just three orbits.''',r'''The algebraic closure and fixed characteristic are indispensable. Arithmetic forms over smaller fields require additional descent data.''','B',r'''The elementary height classification rests on older formal-group theory \citep{lazard1955formal}. The geometric stratification is exactly \citet[Theorem 1]{morava1972moduli}, an unpublished two-page announcement rather than a 1972 journal article; later published support includes \citet[Corollary 1.10]{johnsonwilson1975operations}.''',r'''the height stratification and invariant-prime picture are established. The orbit geometry must be read with its stated base field.''',['lazard1955formal','morava1972moduli','johnsonwilson1975operations'],area='Algebraic topology')
item(268,'P000443','construction','Morava K-theories and the advertised periodicity',r'''The notes announce functors \(K(h)\), with periodicity \(2(p^h-1)\), and describe an infinite-height fibre as ordinary integral cohomology.''',r'''For each prime \(p\) and integer \(h\geq1\), Morava \(K\)-theory is a homology theory with coefficient ring \(K(h)_*=\mathbb F_p[v_h^{\pm1}]\), \(|v_h|=2(p^h-1)\). Multiplication by \(v_h\) is the stated periodicity. Its construction cannot in general be replaced by the naive tensor product \(MU_*(X)\otimes_{MU_*}K(h)_*\). The additive formal group is represented by ordinary cohomology with the chosen coefficients; an infinite-height point in characteristic \(p\) gives a mod-\(p\) formal group, not automatically integral cohomology.''',r'''The coefficient field, grading and distinction between a constructed homology theory and an underived fibre are supplied. The notebook's \(\mathbb Z_p\) notation is not assumed to distinguish \(p\)-adic integers from integers modulo \(p\).''','B',r'''\citet[Introduction, Sections 2--3]{johnsonwilson1975operations} constructs these theories and gives the coefficient ring and period explicitly. Its references identify Morava's Columbia notes as unpublished in 1971; this is earlier circulation distinct from the 1975 publication.''',r'''Morava \(K\)-theories and the periodicity are established. Their heightwise relation to complex bordism has precise hypotheses, not the universal fibre identity suggested by the sketch.''',['johnsonwilson1975operations'],match='partial',area='Algebraic topology')
item(268,'P000443','theorem','Height stabilizers and p-adic division algebras',r'''Adams-type operations are indexed by units in a \(p\)-adic division algebra of dimension \(h^2\), described as the stabilizer of a point of a height orbit.''',r'''For a height-\(h\) one-dimensional formal group over an algebraic closure of \(\mathbb F_p\), its automorphism group is the unit group \(\mathcal O_D^\times\) of the maximal order in the central division algebra \(D/\mathbb Q_p\) of invariant \(1/h\), with \(\dim_{\mathbb Q_p}D=h^2\). The full group \(D^\times\) allows quasi-isogenies and is larger. Stabilizer actions on height-theoretic objects require the chosen coefficient extension and realization; the notebook does not construct every asserted operation.''',r'''Units of the maximal order are distinguished from all nonzero division-algebra elements. The stabilizer identification is verified separately from the stronger claim of an action on a particular integral or mod-\(p\) cohomology theory.''','B',r'''The precise stabilizer is \citet[Theorem 2]{morava1972moduli}; the November 1972 announcement remained unpublished and was later made available from archival material. Morava's published comodule theory gives the broader realization framework \citep{morava1985noetherian}.''',r'''the stabilizer identification is established. A literal \(D^\times\)-action by automorphisms of the formal group would be too large.''',['morava1972moduli','morava1985noetherian'],match='partial',area='Algebraic topology')
item(268,'P000443-P000444','definition','Formal group laws and the Lazard–Quillen universal ring',r'''A one-dimensional commutative formal group law satisfies symmetry, a zero identity and associativity; the inverse exists automatically. The functor of such laws is represented by \(\Omega_U\).''',r'''For a commutative ring \(R\), a formal group law is \(F(x,y)\in R[[x,y]]\) with \(F(x,0)=x\), \(F(0,y)=y\), associativity and commutativity. The linear terms are \(x+y\), so the inverse series is determined recursively. The functor of laws in a chosen coordinate is represented by the Lazard ring \(L\cong\mathbb Z[x_1,x_2,\ldots]\); Quillen identifies its universal law with the complex orientation law on \(MU_*\). The representing scheme classifies laws with coordinates, not isomorphism classes of formal groups.''',r'''The chosen coordinate is made explicit. Modding out coordinate changes leads to a stack rather than replacing the universal law ring by a ring of isomorphism classes.''','E',r'''Representability is Lazard's 1955 theorem \citep{lazard1955formal}; the topological identification is \citet[Theorem 2]{quillen1969formal}, communicated in May and published in November 1969.''',r'''the universal-property statement is established, with the distinction between laws and isomorphism classes.''',['lazard1955formal','quillen1969formal'],correct='correct',area='Algebraic topology')
item(268,'P000445','claim','Invariant differentials determine the logarithm over a Q-algebra',r'''Kazhdan's intervention says that in characteristic zero the invariant differential gives coordinates for the formal group; the coefficient of each power can be prescribed.''',r'''Over a \(\mathbb Q\)-algebra \(R\), a normalized differential \(\omega=a(x)\,dx\), \(a(0)=1\), has a unique integral \(\ell(x)\in R[[x]]\) with \(\ell(0)=0\), \(\ell'(0)=1\). The law \(F(x,y)=\ell^{-1}(\ell(x)+\ell(y))\) has invariant differential \(\omega\). Thus normalized invariant differentials and commutative one-dimensional formal group laws are in bijection over \(\mathbb Q\)-algebras. Characteristic zero alone is insufficient for the integration argument: denominators may be absent, as in \(\mathbb Z\).''',r'''The \(\mathbb Q\)-algebra hypothesis replaces the weaker phrase characteristic zero. The claim is about a chosen coordinate and a normalized differential.''','E',r'''The formula and the necessary rationalization are explicit in \citet[Section 1, formulas (1)--(2)]{quillen1969formal}. Morava returns to it on 14 April.''',r'''the rational classification is established; integral formal groups retain information not removed by a logarithm over the original ring.''',['quillen1969formal'],speaker='D. A. Kazhdan',area='Algebraic topology')
corr('P000440','complex representative','Every weakly complex manifold is cobordant to a complex manifold','Use the verified statement that complex bordism is generated integrally by nonsingular projective varieties; unrestricted actual-representative statement fails in dimension zero','The negatively stably complex point is -1 in MU_0, unlike any complex zero-manifold.');
corr('P000441-P000444','coefficient ring','Z[t_0,t_1,...], degree t_i=2i','Z[t_1,t_2,...]; t_0, if used for the point, is the unit','MU_0=Z, so a free degree-zero polynomial generator cannot occur.');
corr('P000442','dense open fibres','Every fibre over D(t_1) equals integral K^0(X) plus K^1(X)','State the Conner–Floyd base change through the multiplicative formal group; geometric fibres use their residue fields','Already at a point the geometric fibre is a field, not Z. Inverting t_1 alone does not give the K-theory coefficient map.');
corr('P000443','stabilizer','Units of a p-adic division algebra','Units of its maximal order for automorphisms; the whole division-algebra unit group is for quasi-isogenies','Morava 1972, Theorem 2 identifies the stabilizer with E_n units.');
corr('P000445','differential classification','Characteristic zero ring','Q-algebra for unrestricted termwise integration and arbitrary differential coefficients','The logarithm construction divides by positive integers.');save()

