z=byid['1971-262.1']
z['statement']=r'''A locally noetherian scheme has an Artin--Mazur \(\acute{e}\)tale homotopy type in a pro-homotopy category, constructed from connected components of \(\acute{e}\)tale hypercovers. For a connected pointed scheme \(X\) of finite type over \(\mathbb C\), the canonical comparison from the classical analytic homotopy type to \(X_{\acute{e}t}\) induces an isomorphism after profinite completion. If \(X\) is additionally geometrically unibranch---in particular, if \(X\) is smooth or normal---then \(X_{\acute{e}t}\) itself is isomorphic to the profinite completion of the classical type in that pro-homotopy category.'''
z['reconstruction']=r'''The hypotheses on the scheme and its base point are supplied from the primary theorem. Finite type and geometric unibranchness have distinct roles in the two comparison statements. The notebook's informal ``homotopy type'' is not identified with an ordinary integral CW homotopy type.'''
z['fate']=r'''The construction circulated in the Harvard seminar of 1965--1966 and appeared as \citet{artinmazur1969etale}. The precise comparison is Theorem 12.9 and Corollary 12.10, pp. 142--143, read in the full primary text.'''
z['verification']='read the result';z['confidence']='high'
z=byid['1971-262.2'];z['verification']='read the result'
z['fate']=r'''The arithmetic action is already discussed in the introduction, especially p. 4, of \citet{artinmazur1969etale}; its functorial construction belongs to their 1965--1966 seminar work published in 1969. The pointed-versus-outer-action distinction is our explicit formulation of the base-point issue.'''
z=byid['1971-262.3'];z['verification']='read the result'
z['fate']=r'''The pro-category and completion are constructed in \citet[Sections 2--3, pp. 20--34]{artinmazur1969etale}; the complex comparison is Theorem 12.9. This is prior foundational work, not a conjectural completion procedure first proposed in the seminar.'''
z=byid['1971-263.4']
z['title']='Birman’s local trace-class criterion with domain control'
z['statement']=r'''Let \(H,H_0\) be self-adjoint operators on a separable Hilbert space, with spectral projections \(E,E_0\). Assume that, for every bounded interval \(\Delta\), the bounded operator \[H E(\Delta)E_0(\Delta)-E(\Delta)H_0E_0(\Delta)\] belongs to the trace class. Assume also that there exist real Borel functions \(f,f_0\), bounded on compact intervals and satisfying \(|f(\lambda)|,|f_0(\lambda)|\to\infty\) as \(|\lambda|\to\infty\), such that \(D(f(H))=D(f_0(H_0))\). Then both time-dependent wave operators exist and are complete. The domain equality is a sufficient form of the mutual-subordination hypothesis in Birman's theorem.'''
z['reconstruction']=r'''The scan does contain the equality of the two functional-calculus domains and the statement that the functions grow at infinity. Borel measurability, local boundedness and absolute growth are made explicit. The localized difference is written as the bounded expression above so that no undefined subtraction on unequal operator domains is needed. Birman's Definition 4.2 allows one domain inclusion for subordination; mutual subordination follows from the displayed equality.'''
z['class']='E';z['publication_match']='exact';z['verification']='read the result';z['confidence']='high'
z['fate']=r'''This is the local criterion of \citet[Definition 4.2 and Theorem 4.4, pp. 897--900]{birman1968local}, received 23 January 1968 and published that year. Its hypotheses are more general than a globally trace-class difference and explain precisely the final lines of the 27 March lecture.'''
z['present_status']=r'''As of November 2026, this is an established scattering theorem. The notebook's compressed domain condition can be reconstructed from the 1968 source; no separate open extension is justified.'''
z['refs']=['birman1968local']
z=byid['1971-265.11']
z['refs']=['quillen1971cohomology','rouquier2014finite']
z['fate']=r'''Finite generation is already attributed to Venkov and Evens in \citet[Section 1, p. 47]{quillen1971cohomology}. A modern independent proof appears in \citet[Theorem 2.4]{rouquier2014finite}. The faithful embedding need not be irreducible; the regular representation supplies an unconditional embedding of any finite group.'''
