talk(269,'1972-04-14','I. M. Gelfand','P000445-P000446','Dyson’s missed opportunities: eta identities and contractions',r'''Gelfand reports two examples from Dyson's Gibbs Lecture of 17 January 1972: the relation between powers of the Dedekind eta function and root systems, and contractions of kinematical groups. The manuscript preserves the explicit five-variable formula for Ramanujan's tau function and singles out the exponent 26 as unexplained. The missing leading factor in the eta product is a mathematical emendation, while the exceptional exponent has a documented later explanation. The final contraction remark occupies only the top of P000446, before Morava's heading.''')
item(269,'P000445','definition','Ramanujan’s tau function and the missing leading factor',r'''The notebook writes \(\prod_{m=1}^{\infty}(1-x^m)^{24}=\eta^{24}(x)=\sum_{n\geq1}\tau(n)x^n\). The first lower index is faint in transcription, but the scan and Dyson's original use \(m=1\).''',r'''For \(\tau\) in the complex upper half-plane set \(q=e^{2\pi i\tau}\) and \(\eta(\tau)=q^{1/24}\prod_{m\geq1}(1-q^m)\). Ramanujan's integers are defined by \[\Delta(q)=\eta(\tau)^{24}=q\prod_{m\geq1}(1-q^m)^{24}=\sum_{n\geq1}\tau(n)q^n.\] The product without \(q\) has constant term 1 and coefficients \(\tau(n+1)\) at \(q^n\).''',r'''The variable \(q\), the upper-half-plane parameter and the leading factor are restored. The notebook conflates the eta function with its product part; this is not repaired by changing the convention for \(\tau(1)=1\).''','E',r'''The normalization appears explicitly in \citet[equation (1), p. 636]{dyson1972missed}. Dyson delivered the lecture in January 1972; the journal text appeared in September. The missing factor is therefore a notebook error, not a later change of definition.''',r'''the normalization is standard. Its correction follows already by comparing constant terms and is also required by the independent coefficient verification in this chapter.''',['dyson1972missed'],correct='false as written',development='annotated',area='Number theory')
item(269,'P000445','theorem','Dyson’s five-variable lattice formula for tau',r'''The notes sum a ten-factor Vandermonde product over integers \(a,b,c,d,e\) satisfying residues \(1,2,3,4,5\pmod5\), zero sum and square sum \(10n\), with denominator \(1!2!3!4!\).''',r'''For every positive integer \(n\), put \(v=(a,b,c,d,e)\) and \(V(v)=\prod_{1\leq i<j\leq5}(v_i-v_j)\). Then \[\tau(n)=\frac1{288}\!\sum_{\substack{a\equiv1,\ b\equiv2,\ c\equiv3,\ d\equiv4,\ e\equiv0\ (5)\\ a+b+c+d+e=0\\ a^2+b^2+c^2+d^2+e^2=10n}}\! V(a,b,c,d,e).\] Each coordinate is labelled by its residue class; the sum is not divided by an additional permutation factor.''',r'''Residue 5 is written as residue 0, and the denominator is evaluated. The formula has been checked editorially by three exact-integer algorithms for \(1\leq n\leq100\); the computational appendix marks this as \oursComputed{} and does not infer an all-\(n\) proof from the finite check.''','E',r'''The exact identity is \citet[equation (2), p. 636]{dyson1972missed}, already in the January 1972 lecture. Its Lie-theoretic setting is the affine denominator identities discussed by \citet{macdonald1972affine}.''',r'''the identity is established. The notebook's residue order, square-sum scale and denominator agree with the primary formula; the error lies in the preceding generating function, not in this lattice sum.''',['dyson1972missed','macdonald1972affine'],correct='correct',development='annotated',area='Number theory')
item(269,'P000445','claim','Eta powers and products over positive roots',r'''The exponents \(3,8,10,14,15,21,24,26,28,35,36\) are compared with dimensions of simple Lie algebras. The numerator of the tau formula is described as a product over ``simple roots (positive)'' of \(SL(5)\).''',r'''The root system \(A_4\) has ten positive roots \(e_i-e_j\), \(i<j\), and only four simple roots. Evaluating the product over all positive roots gives the ten-factor Vandermonde in the formula above. The Lie-algebra dimensions \(\dim A_1=3\), \(\dim A_2=8\), \(\dim C_2=10\), \(\dim A_4=24\) illustrate the ordinary affine-denominator specializations. The exponent 26 is exceptional to this dimension list and must be considered separately.''',r'''``Simple'' is deleted before ``positive roots''. A denominator-identity family is identified, without asserting that every eta-power identity is uniquely determined by the dimension of a simple Lie algebra.''','E',r'''\citet[pp. 636--639]{dyson1972missed} describes the root-system discovery and Macdonald's general proof. The journal account \citep{macdonald1972affine} appeared in 1972, but the connection was already part of the January lecture summarized here.''',r'''the root-system interpretation is established. Counting ten positive roots versus four simple roots provides an immediate internal check on the manuscript's wording.''',['dyson1972missed','macdonald1972affine'],correct='false as written',match='partial',area='Lie theory')
item(269,'P000445','question','Why does the exponent 26 occur?',r'''The exponent 26 is doubly underlined; Gelfand records that it ``drops out'' of the simple-Lie-algebra dimension pattern and remains unclear.''',r'''Explain the eta-power identity with exponent 26 within the root-system framework, although 26 is not the dimension of a simple complex Lie algebra. The question concerns the structural source of that identity, not the existence of a simple Lie algebra of dimension 26.''',r'''The structural formulation follows the explicit contrast with the surrounding dimension list. No unspecified coefficient identity beyond the one discussed by Dyson is invented.''','B',r'''\citet[Section I, pp. 200--201]{monastyrskii1980appendix} explains the exponent 26 through the dual-root construction associated with \(F_4\). The unequal root lengths lead to a specialization different from the ordinary dimension-52 pattern. This supplies a documented explanation of the notebook's exceptional exponent.''',r'''the exceptional exponent is explained within affine root-system identities. It is not an unresolved problem and does not require a nonexistent 26-dimensional simple Lie algebra.''',['dyson1972missed','monastyrskii1980appendix'],correct='correct',problem='GP-1971-41',area='Lie theory')
problems.append(dict(id='GP-1971-41',title='The exceptional exponent 26 in the eta identities',statement=r'''Explain the \(\eta^{26}\) identity in root-system terms despite the absence of a simple complex Lie algebra of dimension 26.''',occurrences=['1971-269.4'],status=r'''As of November 2026, resolved in the affine-root framework: the dual-root specialization for \(F_4\) is described by \citet[Section I, pp. 200--201]{monastyrskii1980appendix}.''',status_date='2026-11',refs=['dyson1972missed','monastyrskii1980appendix']))
item(269,'P000446','method','Limits of the kinematical groups',r'''The second example consists of \(c\to0\), \(c\to\infty\) and \(R\to\infty\), called limiting transitions in the kinematical group.''',r'''A Lie-algebra contraction chooses invertible linear changes of basis depending on a parameter and takes a finite limit of the transformed structure constants. In the Poincar\'e algebra, normalize boosts by \([K_i,H]=P_i\), \([K_i,P_j]=\delta_{ij}H/c^2\) and \([K_i,K_j]=-\epsilon_{ijk}J_k/c^2\). The limit \(c\to\infty\) gives the Galilei brackets. For \(c\to0\), first set \(C_i=c^2K_i\); then \([C_i,P_j]=\delta_{ij}H\) survives while \([C_i,H]\) and \([C_i,C_j]\) vanish, giving the Carroll limit. Vanishing curvature \(R^{-2}\) contracts a de Sitter algebra to the Poincar\'e algebra.''',r'''The changes of basis and bracket normalizations are supplied to make the shorthand meaningful; limits of groups without specifying coordinates or rescaling are ambiguous. We do not reconstruct a complete classification from this one line.''','E',r'''These are precisely the three processes discussed by \citet[Section 4, pp. 642--643]{dyson1972missed}, which cites Bacry and L\'evy-Leblond's 1968 analysis. Both the contraction method and these examples predate the seminar.''',r'''the contractions are established examples. The excerpt contains no separate conjecture about physical applicability or an unproved classification.''',['dyson1972missed'],match='partial',area='Mathematical physics')
corr('P000445','eta-product generating series','Product (1-x^m)^24 equals sum from n=1 of tau(n)x^n','Insert x before the product; use eta(tau)^24 with x=exp(2 pi i tau)','Scan confirms the omitted factor. Dyson 1972, equation (1) includes x; the unshifted product has constant term 1.');
corr('P000445','root product','all simple roots (positive) of SL(5)','all positive roots of A4','The written numerator has 10 factors; A4 has 10 positive roots but 4 simple roots.');
save()

talk(270,'1972-04-14','Jack Morava','P000446-P000449','Formal groups, invariant differentials and homogeneous orbits',r'''Kirillov first recapitulates the geometric bordism construction and the picture of a bordism module over the coefficient scheme. The discussion then clarifies geometric fibres, coordinate changes, characteristic-zero logarithms and Mishchenko's projective-space coefficients. It ends with a compressed homogeneous-space assertion. The last conclusion is at the top of P000449; P000450--P000451 belong to Gabrielov's 25 September 1972 lecture, outside this record.''')
item(270,'P000446','definition','Kirillov’s recap of bordism cycles over a space',r'''Under the heading ``Kirillov (recapitulation)'', a cycle is a quadruple \((M,\xi,\alpha,f)\): \(M\) is a compact weakly complex manifold, \(\alpha\) is a specified stable tangent-bundle isomorphism, and \(f:M\to X\). A diagram gives the extending bundle and map on a bordism.''',r'''For a CW space \(X\), a geometric complex-bordism cycle consists of a closed smooth manifold with a chosen stable complex structure and a continuous map to \(X\). Two cycles are equivalent when their disjoint union with the opposite bordism structure bounds a compact manifold whose stable complex structure and map extend the boundary data. Disjoint union and Cartesian products give \(MU_*(X)\) its graded \(MU_*\)-module structure.''',r'''``Weak complex set'' in the translation is read as a stably complex manifold. The stable isomorphism is part of the structure and must extend; existence of a complex bundle with the same stable real class alone does not specify a cycle.''','E',r'''This repeats the established geometric construction in 1971-268.1 and 1971-268.4; its pre-seminar context is \citet{connerfloyd1966relation}. The recurrence is an exposition, not a second proposed theory.''',r'''the geometric model and module action are standard. This entry records Kirillov's intervention separately from Morava's later assertions.''',['connerfloyd1966relation'],verification='publisher metadata checked',speaker='A. A. Kirillov',area='Algebraic topology')
item(270,'P000446','theorem','The coefficient ring and the affine sheaf attached to bordism',r'''The coefficient theorem is now written \(A=\Omega_*^U(*)=\mathbb Z[t_1,t_2,\ldots]\), \(\deg t_i=2i\), with \(t_1\leftrightarrow\mathbb {CP}^1\). The module \(M=\Omega_*^U(X)\) gives a sheaf over \(\mathbb A=\operatorname{Spec}A\).''',r'''Complex bordism has coefficient ring \(MU_*\cong\mathbb Z[x_1,x_2,\ldots]\), with \(|x_i|=2i\), and a degree-two generator may be chosen as \([\mathbb {CP}^1]\). Any module \(M\) defines a quasicoherent sheaf \(\widetilde M\) on \(\operatorname{Spec}MU_*\), after forgetting or separately recording its grading. The infinitely generated polynomial coefficient ring is not noetherian.''',r'''This page correctly starts the polynomial generators at index 1, unlike P000441. The nonnoetherian qualification matters when comparing the later finite-type homogeneous-space assertion with the infinite formal-group scheme.''','E',r'''The coefficient theorem and identification with the universal formal-group ring are explicit in \citet[Theorem 2]{quillen1969formal}; the associated-sheaf construction is standard affine algebraic geometry. Morava's equivariant version is developed in \citet[Section 1.0]{morava1985noetherian}.''',r'''the coefficient and sheaf constructions are established. They do not by themselves identify the fibres with familiar integral cohomology theories.''',['quillen1969formal','morava1985noetherian'],correct='correct',speaker='A. A. Kirillov',area='Algebraic topology')
item(270,'P000446-P000447','claim','The three orbit types and the proposed fibre theories',r'''There is said to be one dense open orbit, one closed orbit and countably many intermediate orbits; their fibres are labelled \(K(X)\), \(H^*(X,\mathbb Z)\), and \(K(n)(X,\mathbb Z_p)\). The intermediate labels also involve the prime \(p\).''',r'''Over an algebraically closed field of a fixed positive characteristic \(p\), coordinate-change orbits of one-dimensional commutative formal groups are indexed by heights \(1,2,\ldots,\infty\): height 1 is open, infinite height is closed, and finite higher heights lie between them. The positive finite-height cohomology theories are Morava \(K(h)\), with coefficients \(\mathbb F_p[v_h^{\pm1}]\), \(|v_h|=2(p^h-1)\). These are not a literal classification into three orbits over \(\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z\), nor are all their geometric fibres integral \(K\)-groups or integral ordinary cohomology.''',r'''The orbit theorem is restricted to a fixed algebraically closed base field. The labels of homology theories are separated from raw tensor-product fibres, repeating the corrections in 1971-268.6--1971-268.9. No claim is made that the fibre alone defines a generalized homology theory for every space.''','B',r'''The geometric orbit theorem is \citet[Theorem 1]{morava1972moduli}, an unpublished November 1972 announcement. The coefficient rings and realization of the periodic theories are treated in \citet[Introduction and Sections 2--3]{johnsonwilson1975operations}, citing Morava's unpublished 1971 work.''',r'''the height picture and the periodic theories are established. The global integral-fibre formulation remains false as written, and is not an open problem.''',['morava1972moduli','johnsonwilson1975operations'],correct='false as written',match='partial',area='Algebraic topology')
item(270,'P000447','construction','The fibre M/MJ and the residue field',r'''A point \((a_1,a_2,\ldots)\) gives a purported maximal ideal \(J=(t_1-a_1,t_2-a_2,\ldots)\). The fibre is \(M/MJ\), called a vector space and then more accurately an \(A/J\)-module.''',r'''For an ideal \(J\subset A\), \(M/JM\cong M\otimes_A A/J\) is an \(A/J\)-module. It is a vector space when \(J\) is maximal. The geometric fibre at a prime \(\mathfrak p\) is \(M\otimes_A\kappa(\mathfrak p)\), not in general \(M/\mathfrak p M\). For \(A=\mathbb Z[t_1,t_2,\ldots]\) and integers \(a_i\), the displayed evaluation ideal has quotient \(\mathbb Z\), so it is prime but not maximal; a residue characteristic must also be imposed to obtain the corresponding finite-characteristic point.''',r'''The correction already begun in the notebook is completed. Points lie in \(\operatorname{Spec}A\), not in the module \(M\); an ideal is a subset of \(A\), not a subset of the scheme \(\mathbb A\).''','E',r'''This is the ordinary affine-scheme meaning of fibre. The same coefficient-scheme viewpoint underlies \citet[Section 1.0]{morava1985noetherian}; that later account clarifies the geometry rather than introducing the elementary tensor-product identity.''',r'''the construction is standard. Keeping the base ring prevents a false vector-space assertion and clarifies the varying coefficient fields of the height strata.''',['morava1985noetherian'],correct='false as written',match='partial',area='Algebraic geometry')
item(270,'P000447','definition','Full and strict formal coordinate changes',r'''The group consists of \(g(x)=a_1x+a_2x^2+\cdots\). Both \(a_1=1\) and the condition that \(a_1\) be a unit occur. Laws are equivalent under \(F_1=\varphi^{-1}F_2(\varphi(x),\varphi(y))\).''',r'''For any commutative ring \(R\), series \(g\in xR[[x]]\) with invertible linear coefficient form a group under substitution. The inverse is found recursively. The subgroup with \(g'(0)=1\) consists of strict coordinate changes; over \(\mathbb Z\), the full group permits \(g'(0)=\pm1\). Isomorphism of one-dimensional formal group laws means an invertible series intertwining the laws, equivalently the displayed conjugation formula.''',r'''The two linear-coefficient conditions specify different groups, not interchangeable conventions. The ambiguous margin phrase translated as ``same-form groups'' means isomorphic formal groups in the displayed formula.''','E',r'''Formal changes of coordinate are part of Lazard's pre-seminar theory \citep{lazard1955formal}. Their full/strict distinction and grading are explicit in \citet[Sections 1.0.2--1.0.4]{morava1985noetherian}.''',r'''the construction is established over arbitrary commutative rings, with invertibility required only for the linear coefficient.''',['lazard1955formal','morava1985noetherian'],correct='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Algebraic geometry')
item(270,'P000447','remark','Formal vector fields and the ambiguous dual of differentials',r'''The margin names \(W_1\), the algebra of formal vector fields, and \(W'_1\), a ``dual algebra of differentials'', without specifying the duality or its operations.''',r'''Over a characteristic-zero field \(k\), the formal vector fields are \(W_1=k[[x]]\partial_x\), with \([f\partial_x,g\partial_x]=(fg'-gf')\partial_x\). The module of formal differentials \(k[[x]]\,dx\) is dual to this rank-one module over \(k[[x]]\). This module dual is distinct from the continuous \(k\)-linear dual of the infinite-dimensional Lie algebra. The Lie algebra of origin-fixing formal substitutions is the subalgebra \(xk[[x]]\partial_x\).''',r'''These are precise nearby constructions, not an identification of the unexplained prime on \(W'_1\). No Lie bracket on the differential module, topology for a continuous dual, or claim about a coadjoint representation is supplied by the notes.''','F',r'''The distinctions are explicit in the modern formal-geometry treatment \citet[Section 2.1 and Corollary 2.5.18]{gorbounov2020chiral}. Searches for formal vector fields, dual differentials and Gelfand--Fuks terminology recover that framework, but no exact additional theorem can be assigned to this isolated line.''',r'''the elementary objects are standard, but the intended meaning of ``dual algebra'' remains unidentified in this occurrence. This is a limitation of the record, not evidence of an undeveloped mathematical idea.''',['gorbounov2020chiral'],correct='not determinable',match='partial',confidence='medium',area='Lie theory')
item(270,'P000447-P000448','claim','Associativity and the characteristic-zero commutativity theorem',r'''A law \(F=x+y+\alpha xy+\beta x^2y+\delta xy^2+\cdots\) is said to have associativity as its sole requirement. On the next page, over a field of characteristic zero, it is declared isomorphic to addition and hence commutative.''',r'''Let \(R\) be a \(\mathbb Q\)-algebra and let \(F\in R[[x,y]]\) satisfy \(F(x,0)=x\), \(F(0,y)=y\) and associativity. Writing \(f(x)=\partial_2F(x,0)\), define \(\ell(x)=\int_0^x dt/f(t)\). Then \(\ell(F(x,y))=\ell(x)+\ell(y)\); thus \(F\) is isomorphic to the additive law and is commutative. Over arbitrary rings, associativity and the displayed identity terms define a possibly noncommutative one-dimensional law; commutativity cannot be silently added.''',r'''\ours{} Differentiating associativity in its third variable at zero gives \(f(F(x,y))=\partial_2F(x,y)f(y)\). Consequently the derivative in \(y\) of \(\ell(F(x,y))\) is \(\ell'(y)\); the value at \(y=0\) gives the stated identity. This checks the notebook's logarithm argument without assuming commutativity first. The integral uses division by all positive integers.''','E',r'''Characteristic-zero logarithms are classical formal-group theory \citep{lazard1955formal}; the normalized logarithm and rational coefficient requirement are explicit in \citet[Section 1]{quillen1969formal}. The derivation above is our check of the slightly stronger associative formulation used by the notes.''',r'''the characteristic-zero field assertion is established. Its unrestricted-ring extrapolation is invalid; integral and positive-characteristic formal groups require separate hypotheses.''',['lazard1955formal','quillen1969formal'],match='partial',development='annotated',area='Algebraic geometry')
item(270,'P000448','construction','Invariant differentials, pullback and the obstruction to integration',r'''The invariant differential is \(a(x)dx=dx/f(x)\); its integral gives the inverse coordinate. The substitution rule is \(a(x)\mapsto a(\varphi(x))\varphi'(x)\). The text says any series \(a(x)=1+a_1x+\cdots\) determines a formal group, and notes that \(\int x^{p-1}dx\) does not exist in characteristic \(p\).''',r'''Over a \(\mathbb Q\)-algebra, every \(a(x)\in1+xR[[x]]\) has the unique normalized primitive \(\ell(x)=x+\sum_{n\geq1}a_nx^{n+1}/(n+1)\). The law \(\ell^{-1}(\ell(x)+\ell(y))\) has normalized invariant differential \(a(x)dx\). For a strict change \(\varphi'(0)=1\), pullback sends its coefficient to \(a(\varphi(x))\varphi'(x)\). For a full coordinate change, the normalized differential acquires the additional factor \(1/\varphi'(0)\). In characteristic \(p\), the derivative of every power series has zero coefficient at \(x^{p-1}\), so that monomial has no primitive.''',r'''The scope remains the characteristic-zero field in the preceding theorem, or more generally a \(\mathbb Q\)-algebra. Arbitrary pullback one-forms need not remain normalized. The isolated dot above the second inverse-coordinate symbol is not interpreted as an extra derivative; that reading would contradict the displayed integral.''','E',r'''The invariant differential and logarithm formulas are in \citet[Section 1, equations (1)--(2)]{quillen1969formal}, before the seminar. The substitution factor follows from the usual pullback of one-forms and the choice of normalization.''',r'''the rational correspondence is established. The universal Lazard ring does not remove the positive-characteristic integration obstruction or make every normalized differential over an arbitrary ring arise by this logarithm formula.''',['quillen1969formal'],correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',area='Algebraic geometry')
item(270,'P000448','theorem','Mishchenko’s projective-space coefficients',r'''Morava identifies the coefficients of \(a(x)\) with \([\mathbb {CP}^1],[\mathbb {CP}^2],\ldots\), citing Mishchenko; the page also records a suggestion by Kirillov.''',r'''For the formal group law of the canonical complex orientation of \(MU\), its normalized invariant differential and rational logarithm are \[\omega_F(x)=\left(\sum_{n\geq0}[\mathbb {CP}^{n}]x^n\right)dx,\qquad \log_F(x)=\sum_{n\geq0}\frac{[\mathbb {CP}^{n}]}{n+1}x^{n+1},\] with \([\mathbb {CP}^{0}]=1\). The differential has integral bordism coefficients; the logarithm is taken in \(MU_*\otimes\mathbb Q\), with grading interpreted in the usual complex-oriented convention.''',r'''The denominator belongs in the logarithm, not in the differential. A complex orientation and its coordinate are fixed; arbitrary polynomial generators of \(MU_*\) are not being equated with every projective-space class.''','E',r'''\citet[Corollary to Theorem 1]{quillen1969formal} explicitly credits Mishchenko and gives the logarithm formula. Its differential is exactly the coefficient assertion recorded here, already published in 1969.''',r'''the formula is established. It connects geometric projective-space classes with the formal-group logarithm and supplies a precise bridge between the two parts of the lecture.''',['quillen1969formal'],correct='correct',area='Algebraic topology')
item(270,'P000448-P000449','claim','When a transitive group-scheme action is a homogeneous quotient',r'''The final theorem assumes a smooth \(G\)-scheme \(X\) over \(\mathbb Z\), a flat group scheme \(G\), noetherian hypotheses, and transitivity of \(G(K)\) on \(X(K)\) for an algebraically closed field. The continuation asserts \(X\cong G/S\), with \(S\) a stabilizer.''',r'''A precise field version is the following. Let \(k\) be algebraically closed, \(G\) a smooth affine algebraic group over \(k\), and \(X\) a nonempty separated reduced finite-type \(k\)-scheme on which \(G(k)\) acts transitively. For \(x\in X(k)\), the scheme-theoretic stabilizer \(S=G_x\) is closed and the orbit map identifies \(X\) with the faithfully flat quotient \(G/S\). The stabilizer need not be reduced. Over a base such as \(\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z\), a corresponding quotient description needs a global section and a faithfully flat orbit map. Geometric-point transitivity alone does not supply those data.''',r'''The verified statement is explicitly restricted to a field. The manuscript's quantifier before \(K\) is crossed out, and geometric-fibre transitivity does not itself provide a chosen global section or a globally defined stabilizer over \(\mathbb Z\). Nor do finite-type noetherian hypotheses automatically apply to the infinite-dimensional formal-coordinate group used earlier.''','E',r'''The orbit and quotient theorem is established algebraic-group theory; see \citet[Propositions 7.11--7.17, especially Remark 7.16(c)]{milne2017groups}. This gives a verified precise core, not verification of the stronger relative formulation exactly as abbreviated in the notebook.''',r'''the field theorem and the faithfully flat orbit criterion are established. The intended full relative theorem remains underdetermined by the cancelled quantifier and missing section data.''',['milne2017groups'],correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',confidence='medium',area='Algebraic geometry')
