talk(288,'1973-02-19','R. L. Dobrushin','P000480','Nelson’s Euclidean Markov-field construction',r'''The manuscript explicitly dates this short record 19 February 1973, although the catalogue supplied with the task says 19 February 1972. The YEAR1973 heading and adjacent Buchstaber dates support a catalogue typo. It is retained among the 45 requested records but uses the corrected academic-year prefix 1972. Gelfand distinguishes quantizing given equations from finding physically adequate equations; Dobrushin then identifies Nelson's probabilistic Euclidean-field method and says that Glimm--Jaffe results can ``almost'' be recovered. Only P000480 belongs to this talk: P000479 and P000481 are parts of different Buchstaber lectures.''')
item(288,'P000480','problem','Gelfand’s programme of quantizing existing equations',r'''Gelfand's first task is \ru{проквантовать существующ. ур-я}: quantize the existing equations.''',r'''No specific classical equation, phase space, quantization rule, dimension or required quantum theory is supplied. The line therefore states a research programme; it cannot be converted into a definite existence or uniqueness question without adding its mathematical object and success criterion.''',r'''The neighbouring Nelson discussion suggests constructive quantum field theory as context, but does not determine whether Gelfand intended a particular polynomial interaction or a canonical quantization prescription.''','F',r'''Contemporary context is supplied by \citet[Introduction and Section 1]{cartier1974quantum}, whose November 1972 lecture discusses explicit scalar-field equations and the gap between formal equations and construction. That is thematic evidence, not an exact match for the unspecified equation in this occurrence.''',r'''the problem cannot be identified at the level required for an open-problem entry. No claim about its solution, insolubility or lack of later work follows from this shorthand.''',['cartier1974quantum'],correct='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='indirect',confidence='low',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',area='Mathematical physics')
item(288,'P000480','problem','Gelfand’s programme of finding physically adequate equations',r'''The second task is to write equations that really correspond to physics; no model or observable is named.''',r'''This is a request to choose a physically adequate dynamical model. The notes contain no mathematical class of equations, data to be reproduced, accuracy criterion, or existence assertion. A precise modern formulation cannot be supplied from this record alone.''',r'''The distinction from the first task is preserved: constructing a quantum theory for a specified classical model and selecting that model are different questions. Neither is identified with a later named conjecture.''','F',r'''The model-construction discussion in \citet[Section 1.5]{cartier1974quantum} provides contemporary background. Searches centered on Dobrushin, Nelson and the listed date did not identify a particular equation or claim corresponding to Gelfand's second programme.''',r'''the intended mathematical problem remains unidentified. This is not evidence that a definite 1973 conjecture remains open.''',['cartier1974quantum'],correct='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',area='Mathematical physics')
item(288,'P000480','definition','Euclidean-invariant random fields and the spatial Markov property',r'''Dobrushin writes random quantities \(\xi_t\), indexed by \(t\in\mathbb R^{\nu+1}\), satisfying Euclidean invariance and the Markov property.''',r'''A random generalized scalar field is a continuous linear map \(T:\mathcal S(\mathbb R^d)\to L^0(\Omega,\mathbb P)\), where \(L^0\) carries convergence in probability. Euclidean invariance means equality of joint laws after every Euclidean isometry. Let \(\mathcal F(U)\) be generated by field tests supported in an open region \(U\), and choose a specified boundary sigma-field \(\mathcal F(\partial U)\). The spatial Markov property says that, for integrable \(\mathcal F(U)\)-measurable \(Z\), \(\mathbb E[Z\mid\mathcal F(U^c)]=\mathbb E[Z\mid\mathcal F(\partial U)]\). This concerns conditional independence of spatial regions, not merely a one-parameter Markov process.''',r'''Generalized fields replace pointwise random variables because the latter need not exist. The boundary sigma-field is part of the definition: in the Sobolev formulation below it is generated by the extended field evaluated on supported \(H^{-1}\) distributions. It must not automatically be replaced by an intersection of neighbourhood sigma-fields.''','E',r'''This is Nelson's framework, already described in \citet[Section 3.1 and note (3), p. 27]{cartier1974quantum}, a November 1972 lecture published in 1974. Nelson's own bibliography dates the initial Berkeley exposition to August 1971, with proceedings publication in 1973 \citep{nelson1973quantum}.''',r'''the framework is established. The notebook's two labels identify the method but do not specify the analytic regularity needed for a reconstruction theorem.''',['cartier1974quantum','nelson1973quantum'],match='partial',area='Probability and mathematical physics')
item(288,'P000480','method','Nelson reconstruction with explicit analytic assumptions',r'''The main author of a probabilistic method for constructing objects satisfying the Wightman axioms is identified as Nelson. Only Euclidean invariance and a Markov property are written.''',r'''One precise version, in four Euclidean dimensions, starts with a real random tempered distribution \(T\) whose law is invariant under all Euclidean isometries, including reflection. Assume that \(T\) extends continuously to \(H^{-1}(\mathbb R^4)\). For closed \(F\), let \(\mathcal F(F)\) be generated by \(T(u)\) with \(u\in H^{-1}\) supported in \(F\), and impose the spatial Markov property with these sigma-fields. Conditional expectation and time translation then give a strongly continuous self-adjoint contraction semigroup \(e^{-tH}\), \(H\geq0\), on \(L^2(\Omega,\mathcal F(\{t=0\}),\mathbb P)\). Put \(\phi_0(u)=T(u\otimes\delta_0)\). Assume in addition that some integer \(m\geq0\) satisfies \[\int |\phi_0(u)|\,|\Phi|^2\,d\mathbb P<\infty\quad\text{for all }u\in\mathcal S(\mathbb R^3),\ \Phi\in D(H^m).\] Nelson's construction then yields smeared fields on the smooth domain of \(H\); restricting to the cyclic vacuum subspace gives temperedness, Poincar\'e covariance, the spectrum condition and local commutativity. Uniqueness of the invariant vacuum is not asserted without an additional ergodicity or clustering hypothesis.''',r'''The dimension-four restriction, Sobolev extension, supported-distribution boundary fields and energy-domain condition are taken from the contemporary precise account, not from the two notebook bullets. The displayed integrability condition contains the first power of \(|\phi_0(u)|\); this was checked visually on p. 20. The statement is not the assertion that Euclidean invariance plus an arbitrary Markov property automatically implies all Wightman axioms.''','E',r'''The construction was presented in Nelson's 1971 Berkeley lecture and published in \citet{nelson1973quantum,nelson1973construction}. The detailed formulation above is read in \citet[Sections 3.2--3.4, pp. 19--23, and note (3)]{cartier1974quantum}, whose lecture predates this corrected February 1973 occurrence. The later Osterwalder--Schrader approach has its own hypotheses and a documented 1975 correction \citep{osterwalderschrader1975euclidean}.''',r'''Nelson reconstruction is established with explicit analytic assumptions. This entry identifies and delimits the method; it does not claim that the notebook specifies a new interacting model or verifies those assumptions for one.''',['nelson1973quantum','nelson1973construction','cartier1974quantum','osterwalderschrader1975euclidean'],correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',confidence='high',area='Mathematical physics')
item(288,'P000480','claim','The unspecified recovery of Glimm–Jaffe results',r'''The notebook says that one can ``almost'' obtain the results of Glimm and Jaffe by the probabilistic method, without naming those results.''',r'''No interaction polynomial, spacetime dimension, cutoff regime, limit or axiom verified is supplied. Consequently there is no definite theorem to compare with the Glimm--Jaffe constructions. A plausible historical context is the use of Euclidean methods in two-dimensional interacting scalar-field theory, but that context does not identify the word ``almost'' mathematically.''',r'''No full construction in four dimensions or assertion of all Wightman axioms for a specified nonlinear equation is inferred. The modern statement remains a delimited identification problem.''','F',r'''\citet[Section 3.5, pp. 23--24]{cartier1974quantum} discusses Nelson's relation to truncated two-dimensional Glimm--Jaffe Hamiltonians and Nelson symmetry. Its note added in November 1973 records further measure constructions, showing why talk date and publication date must be separated. These are plausible nearby results, not an exact match for the unqualified notebook line.''',r'''the exact referenced result and omitted qualification remain unidentified in this audit. Established constructive-field literature exists; the absence of a precise notebook statement gives no basis for class C or D.''',['cartier1974quantum','nelson1973construction'],correct='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='indirect',confidence='low',area='Mathematical physics')
